WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.767
What changed in 2026.

2
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:06.736
Companies stopped paying senior money for simple API wrappers.

3
00:00:06.736 --> 00:00:08.867
Let's see what they pay for instead.

4
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:11.053
Here is the shift.

5
00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:13.300
On the left, what used to impress people.

6
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.167
Wrapping an OpenAI API is now easy to find.

7
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.800
So are LangChain scripts living in a notebook.

8
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.468
The premium goes to engineers who build real systems around the model.

9
00:00:24.468 --> 00:00:27.400
That old skill is abundant, this one gets paid.

10
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.232
So how do you define the job in one line?

11
00:00:31.232 --> 00:00:38.167
A twenty five lakh a year AI engineer is a distributed systems engineer who works with probabilistic models.

12
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:43.767
They wrap predictable, deterministic control layers around unpredictable LLMs.

13
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:48.100
And they cut cost, prevent hallucination, and guarantee reliability.

14
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.600
Postings keep asking for four things.

15
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.559
First, stateful systems.

16
00:00:53.559 --> 00:01:00.400
They name LangGraph for cyclic agent graphs, and MCP for connecting to company databases and tools.

17
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.114
Second, async Python.

18
00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:07.367
LLM apps wait on the network, so you must handle hundreds of requests at once.

19
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:08.978
Third, evals first.

20
00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:14.500
Automated tests for accuracy, hallucination rate and latency, before you deploy.

21
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.033
And fourth, you own cost and latency.

22
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:19.967
Here is the fourth skill in action.

23
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:21.567
A request comes in.

24
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:23.800
A router decides how hard it is.

25
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.400
Easy work goes to a small, cheap model.

26
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.188
Hard reasoning goes to a frontier model.

27
00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:32.733
Prompt caching and semantic caching cut the bill further.

28
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:35.767
One rule ties it all together.

29
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:38.396
If you can't measure it, you can't ship it.

30
00:01:38.396 --> 00:01:39.967
Evals come before deployment.

31
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.012
Let's recap.

32
00:01:42.012 --> 00:01:44.267
Wrapping an API is abundant now.

33
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:47.367
The premium goes to distributed systems thinking.

34
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.000
That means stateful, async, evals first, and cost aware.

35
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.862
And you send each task to the right model.

36
00:01:53.862 --> 00:01:57.767
Next up: Tier one, what every twenty five lakh posting asks for.
